warm.body:
Kool DJ Red Alert has announced his radio retirement after over four decades on the airwaves.
On Monday (Nov. 2), the legendary DJ and hip-hop pioneer made the announcement in an Instagram post, revealing he&#x27;ll be playing a four-hour set as his &quot;farewell broadcast&quot; on Nov. 30 on his current radio home, 107.5 WBLS in New York City.
&quot;Today I stand before you to announce that I&#x27;m saying farewell to radio,&quot; Red Alert said in a video. &quot;It has been an honor in my life to share the airwaves with the community for over four decades.&quot;
He continued: &quot;Thank you for being a part of my journey. The love, the memories, the music and the connection we all have shared and will always hold a special place in my heart. Although I am stepping away from radio, my passion for music and entertainment remains strong. I will continue to bring the sounds, the energy and the vibe to the people through DJing special events and community celebrations. Radio may be one chapter, but the music and the entertainment will continue. Respect.&quot;
Red Alert, who turns 70 on Nov. 27, has been on the turntables for 50 years. In the 1970s, the Harlem native helped popularize mixtapes when he became one of the first DJs to record mix compilation albums on cassette tapes. Alert himself credits DJs Brucie Bee and Starchild with the mixtape trends that followed. Alert&#x27;s radio career began shortly after in the mid-1980s on New York&#x27;s 98.7 KISS-FM, where he then became the first mixshow DJ to bring dancehall to mainstream radio. He later moved over to New York&#x27;s Hot97 in the 1990s when the format flipped to hip-hop, and then moved on to WBLS, where he has been since.

cold.body:
BigXThaPlug was quick to say yes when asked if he feels rap music is dying, and he insists drill music is one of the leading causes.
In an interview on the Club Shay Shay podcast that premiered on Oct. 28, host Shannon Sharpe was surprised at how fast X replied, &quot;Yes,&quot; when asked about the death of the genre. The Texas native then explained his reasoning.
&quot;I been rapping five years. Ain&#x27;t no way I should be at the top,&quot; X said. &quot;There&#x27;s no way somebody that got a TikTok song should have the number one hip-hop song in the world. And you got people who been rapping their whole life, who been in this game forever and know the ins and outs, and you&#x27;re in the same boat as Future. How does that work? It&#x27;s people who ain&#x27;t put in a lick of work in the same category as Drake. The hip-hop game I feel like now is a money game.&quot;
BigX went on to say country music is really flourishing right now, but that even people within the genre said country took a hit and is just now bouncing back. When asked how hip-hop could do the same, he had some ideas.
&quot;It need to be fun again,&quot; X said. &quot;I feel like it ain&#x27;t fun no more. And no disrespect to the drill rappers and you&#x27;re not gonna be making diss songs [at me], but when the drill music came out, it kind of I feel like... They was killing other things, but they was killing hip-hop, too. It definitely did something different to it and now I feel like everybody feel like they gotta be aggressive. They gotta be a gangsta.&quot;
